00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:40.040
To remind you, what was a half life?

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:45.270
The half life of a radioisotope is the
time for the radiation level to decrease,

00:17:45.499 --> 00:17:48.770
or decay, half of its original amount.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:53.229
So every single radioisotope has
a time frame where that occurs.

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:58.010
So what that means, after every half
life, it's going to decrease by half.

00:17:58.409 --> 00:18:03.600
So if I started 100 percent on my sample,
I'll have 50, then I'll have 25, then

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:06.730
I'll have 12 and a half, and so forth,
and it'll keep continuing, right?

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:10.810
It'll be half, a quarter, an eighth,
and so forth, a sixteenth, and so forth

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:13.010
for every single half life that passes.

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:15.090
It's going to be very,
very little in the end.

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:18.280
So let's try to solve this problem
that we were just looking at.

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:21.070
So the first thing that we got
to do is we have to write out,

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:23.010
well, how much sample did we have?

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:27.260
And it says that we have
64 micrograms of sample.

00:18:27.659 --> 00:18:31.459
So what we're going to do is we're going
to write out our half lives and try to

00:18:31.459 --> 00:18:35.240
see, well, when do we get to 136 minutes?

00:18:35.689 --> 00:18:39.249
So the one thing is that you
could do is we could, uh, simply

00:18:39.249 --> 00:18:41.409
just take that 136 minutes.

00:18:41.814 --> 00:18:48.314
And then see if it divides cleanly into
our half lifetime, which was 68 minutes.

00:18:48.705 --> 00:18:51.345
So if we do that, right,
we see we have 68 minutes.

00:18:51.345 --> 00:18:55.794
We see that our total
time pass was 136 minutes.

00:18:56.235 --> 00:18:58.334
So all these things can be used.

00:18:58.784 --> 00:19:03.235
So if it divides cleanly, then we
