There are units when it comes to actually
talking about radiation for when we're

00:20:53.605 --> 00:20:55.545
trying to understand some of the basics.

00:20:55.545 --> 00:20:56.815
I wrote some of them here.

00:20:57.294 --> 00:20:59.044
The first one is the activity.

00:20:59.054 --> 00:21:03.175
So when we talk about activity, we
usually look at it in two different units.

00:21:03.565 --> 00:21:08.555
The common unit is a Curie named after
Marie Curie, the woman who founded and

00:21:08.555 --> 00:21:12.895
won the Nobel Prize for radiation and
discovered multiple radioactive elements.

00:21:13.325 --> 00:21:18.375
So what it means is one curie tells
you how many times an isotope is

00:21:18.375 --> 00:21:20.885
disintegrating over a course of a second.

00:21:21.315 --> 00:21:24.794
So usually it's always how much
is, uh, a gram disintegrates

00:21:24.795 --> 00:21:27.485
within a second is usually the
way that's always referred to as.

00:21:27.905 --> 00:21:31.615
So what we say is one curie is equal to 3.

00:21:31.615 --> 00:21:35.085
7 times 10 to the 10
disintegrations per second.

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:39.510
Now, there's also the SI
unit, which is a Becquerel.

00:21:39.909 --> 00:21:45.069
So whenever we use a Becquerel, it's a
Bq, and then one Becquerel is equal to 3.

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:48.389
7 times 10 to the 10
disintegrations per second.

00:21:49.269 --> 00:21:51.519
Um, there's also absorbed doses.

